Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and their potential usage  
• `event_name` : filter only the “session_start” events that define a visit/session  
• `event_date` : string in format YYYYMMDD – lets us place every event in a calendar day and, by truncation, in a calendar week starting Monday  
• `user_pseudo_id` : anonymous user id – used to count/distinct users for the cohort and for retention checks  
• `user_first_touch_timestamp` : µ‑seconds from Unix epoch – converted to date to know when the user was first seen; if that date equals `event_date` the user is “new” for that cohort week  
• `_TABLE_SUFFIX` (BigQuery auto‑column for wildcard tables) : limits the scan to the days we really need (20180709–20181002)  

Typical conditions  
• `event_name = 'session_start'`  
• `_TABLE_SUFFIX BETWEEN '20180709' AND '20181002'`  
• `event_date = FORMAT_DATE('%Y%m%d', DATE(TIMESTAMP_MICROS(user_first_touch_timestamp)))` (makes sure the visit is on the first‑touch day)  
• `DATE_TRUNC(PARSE_DATE('%Y%m%d', event_date), WEEK(MONDAY))` to derive the cohort‑week start date  
• Filter cohorts `BETWEEN '2018-07-09' AND '2018-09-17'` (they are the only ones that can have a full 2‑week look‑ahead in the public data).  

